,314,1439,13173,11,353,4110,6051,310,1438,0,248046],"sampler":{"temperature":0.0,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0,"min_p":0.0,"seed":0},"max_new_tokens":4096,"decoded_text":"I cannot solve the Riemann Hypothesis.\n\nThe Riemann Hypothesis is one of the most famous unsolved problems in mathematics. It was proposed by Bernhard Riemann in 1859 and remains unproven despite over 160 years of effort by the world’s leading mathematicians. It is one of the seven Millennium Prize Problems, with a $1 million prize offered by the Clay Mathematics Institute for a correct proof or disproof.\n\n### Why it’s unsolved:\n- **Depth and Complexity**: The hypothesis connects the distribution of prime numbers (the building blocks of arithmetic) to the zeros of the Riemann zeta function, a complex analytic function. This bridge between number theory and complex analysis is profound and not yet fully understood.\n- **No Known Path**: Despite extensive numerical verification (trillions of zeros have been checked and all lie on the critical line Re(s) = 1/2), no general proof exists. Many attempted proofs have been published, but all have contained errors or gaps.\n- **Mathematical Consensus**: The mathematical community widely believes the hypothesis is true, but belief is not proof. A rigorous, logically complete argument is required.\n\n### What I *can* do:\n- Explain the statement of the Riemann Hypothesis in detail.\n- Discuss its implications for number theory, cryptography, and physics.\n- Summarize historical attempts and current research directions.\n- Provide code to compute zeros of the zeta function numerically.\n\nIf you’d like to explore any of these aspects, I’m happy to help!<|im_end|>","stop_reason":"stop_token","execution":{"requested_prefill":"auto","effective_prefill":"serial","schedule_basis":"sweep_source_plan","numerical_relationship":"serial_reference","minimum_span_tokens":65,"chunk_cap_tokens":1024,"scratch_priced_upper_bytes":0,"packed_spans":[],"serial_reason":"no_eligible_